  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 117 AM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nAn upper-level ridge across the central portion of the country and\ndownstream low pressure/troughing over the East Coast has our \nforecast area beneath northwest flow aloft. This should keep the \narea dry headed into midweek and allow for daytime highs to nudge \nover the 90 degree mark. Mid- to late-week though, the pattern \nwill break down. The trailing end of the trough may cut off along \nthe Gulf Coast or near the Southeast US, while the ridge is \nsqueezed eastward. A low pressure system wobbling through the \nSouthwest US and remnants of current East Pacific Hurricane Polo \nwill be wrapping plenty of moisture into the Desert Southwest \nbefore nudging eastward.\n\nGuidance trends remain on the slower range with any eastward \nmovement, but some rain is possible in our region as the pattern \nevolves mostly Wednesday night and onward. Some guidance struggles\nto bring the system or its cold front into our forecast at all, \nbut in the same time an easterly wave arriving from the Tropics \nwill bring a surge of moisture northward from the Gulf and \nCaribbean. This is nudging confidence higher for more widespread \nrain chances around next weekend. Generally expect heavy rain to \nbe possible with PWAT values exceeding 2 inches - even more \nnotable for early October. It's too early to even have confidence\nin \"average\" rainfall amounts in the area, but upper-end \nestimates indicate a 90 percent chance for multi-day rainfall to \nbe less than 4 inches for any location in our area. To put it in \nother terms, it might be expected that 10 percent of the region \ncould receive those kinds of rainfall amounts, and 90 percent \nmight see less based on current available guidance. Will continue \nto monitor trends and update messaging as this system approaches \nand evolves. /NF/",
